kory-parser is an independent experimental Python implementation.
It is not the official kordoc Python SDK and does not invoke JavaScript while parsing.

Porting reference: kordoc 4.22.15, https://github.com/chrisryugj/kordoc
Copyright (c) 2026 chrisryugj. MIT license, reproduced in LICENSE.
Adapted portions: HWP5 record/control decoding, addressed table assembly,
HWP3 paragraph/control decoding, Johab and Hancom character mapping tables.
Modifications: Python rewrite, dataclass contracts, explicit source references,
bounded input handling and a differential comparison runner.

Version 0.2.0 additionally adapts clip rectangle adjacency/containment and visible
table band reconstruction from src/pdf/clip-cells.ts and src/table/layout-frames.ts
of kordoc 4.22.15. These modules are attributed to kordoc under its MIT license.

The HWP algorithms and character tables in kordoc in turn derive from rhwp:
https://github.com/edwardkim/rhwp
Copyright edwardkim. MIT license, reproduced in third_party/rhwp_license.txt.

The PDF implementation uses pdfplumber/pdfminer and PDFium for primitives and
includes the above clip/layout adaptations. It is not a complete port of
kordoc's PDF.js/OpenDataLoader-based table detection. Dependencies retain their
own licenses. OCR is an optional RapidOCR integration, not identical to kordoc OCR.

Version 0.3.0 adds single-pass pdfminer decoding, indexed rule coverage and
form/page continuation reconstruction. Optional PyMuPDF integration calls the
installed library API; no PyMuPDF source or binary is vendored in this package.
PyMuPDF / MuPDF is offered under GNU AGPL v3 or an Artifex commercial license:
https://pymupdf.io/licensing . The pdf_native extra is optional and explicit.
fontTools, used to inspect embedded glyph outlines, retains its own MIT license.

Version 0.4.0 preserves pdfplumber 0.11.10 glyph clustering semantics while
replacing quadratic source-order lookup, and indexes glyph centers for cell
selection. It uses MuPDF's compact drawing tuples to avoid temporary geometry
wrappers. No parsed document result is cached or reused across parse calls.

Version 0.5.0 adds source-checked decorative-strip and duplicate-grid filtering,
PDF typography-based headings and lettered/three-page cell continuation with
page-region preservation. OCR changes are excluded from this release's evaluation.

Version 0.6.0 streams worksheet XML to discard formatting-only cells, joins
repeated-header numbered PDF tables and removes text/shading-derived duplicate
grids inside complete ruled forms. Two-field ruled headers can coalesce invisible
value/unit clipping columns while retaining original fragment coordinates.
Evaluation uses newly downloaded public bid attachments, without OCR.

Version 0.7.0 uses MuPDF as the only PDF interpreter and OCR rasterizer.
PyMuPDF and fontTools are required dependencies; pdfplumber, pdfminer.six and
PDFium are no longer runtime dependencies. Historical optional-extra notices
above describe versions through 0.6.0. PyMuPDF retains its AGPL/commercial terms.

Coordinate-only word grouping and ruled-cell reconstruction in pdf_words.py and
pdf_rules.py adapt pdfplumber 0.11.10 (MIT), Copyright (c) 2015 Jeremy Singer-Vine.
Its license is reproduced in third_party/pdfplumber_license.txt. No pdfplumber or
pdfminer PDF interpreter is vendored. Reviewed font-outline digests contain no
font binaries; their source and visual-review evidence are recorded in
verification/pdf_glyph_review_v0_7_0.json.
